<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      CarPlay.

      This entitlement is granted by Apple per app, on request, not by
      having a paid developer account. A build signed without it will
      compile, install, and show nothing in a car - with no error
      anywhere, because the scene simply never connects.

      Request it at:
        https://developer.apple.com/contact/carplay/

      "communication" is the category HyperLink fits: it is messaging,
      the templates it uses are the messaging templates, and it does not
      claim to be navigation, audio or parking. Asking for the wrong
      category is the usual reason a request is refused.

      This file is NOT applied unconditionally. ios/scripts/prepare_project.py
      injects CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS only when the build is told it has
      the grant (pass the carplay flag, or set
      HNX_CARPLAY_ENTITLEMENT=1), because
      shipping a restricted entitlement you do not hold does not degrade
      gracefully.

      This comment used to say the opposite. It claimed a build without
      the grant "will compile, install, and show nothing in a car - with
      no error anywhere", and the project setting was unconditional on
      the strength of that. It is wrong. A restricted entitlement has to
      be in the provisioning profile; an app signed with one that is not
      is killed at launch by amfid - EXC_CRASH (SIGKILL), termination
      reason CODESIGNING - before any of its own code runs. Not CarPlay
      missing: the app does not open.

      This gating is a precaution, not a fix for anything observed.
      It was added while chasing a crash-on-launch and the first
      explanation was that this entitlement caused it. That turned out
      to be wrong: an unsigned archive carries no entitlements file at
      all, the shipped IPA is unsigned, so this never reached a phone
      and cannot have killed anything. The reasoning above still holds
      for a build that *is* signed without the grant, which is why the
      gating stays.

      Sources/CarPlay is behind `#if canImport(CarPlay)` and the scene
      manifest entry is genuinely inert without the entitlement, so a
      build without it is a complete app minus the car.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.carplay-communication</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
